A||||4||ChadMan||Rebuild brake calipers?||||1
Z||000000||ChadMan||11-27-1999||07:50 PM||||While rotating my tires today and examining my brake pads, I noticed that my left rear brake caliper on my 88 Coupe has a small split in the rubber piston seal.  I don't see any evidence of fluid leakage, but it scares me anyway.  The brakes work fine - nice firm pedal.<P>How tough is it to rebuild the calipers?  The Fiero Store wants $140/side for rebuilt calipers, but only $25 for the rebuild kit, which includes the seal I need.  (I once rebuilt the wheel cylinders on an old Duster with 4-wheel drums, but I suspect the Fiero calipers would be harder.)<P>Thanks in advance for everybody's advice.||||reg||1
Z||000001||TOM||11-27-1999||08:38 PM||||The part you are refering to is not the actual seal. It is the dust boot. It is there to keep sand,water etc from getting in and damaging the true seal. If you clean it off you could put a dab of silicone on it. Or get the rebuild kit and just change the boot and save the rest till it really needs a rebuild.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||11-27-1999||11:23 PM||||most of us generaly don't advocate inexperienced people rebuilding rear calipers. It is better to replace them. In your case the easy fix is like tom said. You can't go to wrong with either idea.<P>check your local parts store. They likely have the kits the same price or cheaper and no shipping. same for the caliper as a whole when it finally quits.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ChadMan||11-28-1999||08:55 AM||||Thanks for the tips, guys.  I'd hate to spend $280 replacing calipers that really weren't shot yet from a functional standpoint.<P>How's the new job working out, Ogre?||||reg||1
Z||000004||Raydar||11-28-1999||09:34 PM||||I'm in the process of replacing the rear calipers on my 88. (One was leaking. Replacing the pair.) Advance Auto (not Auto Zone, as I stated before) has rebuilt rear calipers for approximately $75. I think the fronts are a bit cheaper. You will need to transfer *everything*. It includes the back half of the caliper/piston assembly. Oh, it also includes the bleeder fitting, the nut for the parking brake bracket, and the washers for the brake line. No sliders. No bridge. No front half. No pins. No springs. No pads. But for $75... <BR>They also have Bendix calipers, but they were *much* more expensive.<p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 11-30-1999).]||||reg||1
